  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical findings are based on a single-city study of Edinburgh, which has a highly historic and complex urban landscape that may not represent modern or grid-based transit environments.",
      "what": "This study used a mixed-methods approach to analyze public transport accessibility in Edinburgh, combining spatio-temporal machine learning analyses of live-recorded transit data with qualitative thematic analysis of semi-structured interviews with visually impaired elderly residents. The analysis mapped network coverage, service patterns, and density profiles, while the interviews explored spatial cognition, memory-based navigation, and the use of assistive technology.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that while vulnerable elderly residents are willing to adopt artificial intelligence and digital navigation tools, their independence is restricted by a highly centralized transit network, a lack of real-time information in accessible formats, and a systemic failure to collect and store granular accessibility data for physical facilities."
    }
  },
